# Docker Compose configuration — copy to `.env` before `docker compose up`.
# Compose reads this file automatically from the project root.

# REQUIRED. Signs every JWT the API issues. The compose file provides no
# default, so the stack fails to start rather than run on a placeholder.
# Generate a strong value:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=

# REQUIRED. MongoDB credentials. The compose file refuses to interpolate
# without all four, so the stack can never come up credential-free.
#
#   The root user is created in the admin database on a brand-new volume
#   and is used only by the healthcheck (and by humans doing maintenance).
#   The application user gets readWrite on the pos database and is what
#   api, file and the backup service connect as — never root.
#
#   Both passwords are left blank on purpose. A placeholder here would
#   satisfy the nonempty check above and leave a published password
#   guarding real sales. Generate one per password:
#     openssl rand -hex 16
#
#   Letters and digits only, because the README runbook interpolates the
#   values into a mongosh program. The scripts read them from the
#   environment instead, so only the runbook depends on that.
#
#   Enabling auth never creates these users on an already-populated
#   volume: see the runbook "Enabling auth on an existing host" in README.
MONGO_ROOT_USERNAME=pos_root
MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD=
MONGO_APP_USER=pos_app
MONGO_APP_PASSWORD=

# Optional. Database the application user is created in and authenticates
# against; the services connect to this same database.
# MONGO_APP_DATABASE=pos

# Optional. Host port the database is published on; always bound to
# 127.0.0.1 (see docker-compose.yml), so it is reachable locally only.
# MONGO_HOST_PORT=27017

# Optional. Backup scheduling for the `backup` service (see README).
# BACKUP_DIR is a host directory, outside the docker volumes; compose
# creates it relative to this file. No spaces in the path.
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=21600   # every 6 hours
# BACKUP_KEEP=14                  # most recent archives of each kind

# Optional. Address the two services bind on the host. The default publishes
# on every interface, which is what development wants; a deployment behind a
# TLS proxy on the same machine should use 127.0.0.1, and a shop whose
# terminals talk to the API directly should name the one LAN address it wants.
# API_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0
# FILE_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0

# Optional. QR payments. PAYMENT_PROVIDER chooses the gateway the same
# payment routes talk to: `cutluy` (https://cutluy.com — an API in front of
# ABA KHQR, USD only, sandbox-free) or `payway` (ABA Bank's own PayWay, KHR or
# USD, needs an approved sandbox account). It defaults to cutluy. With no
# credentials the stack still starts and the payment routes answer 503 naming
# the variables that are missing, so this whole block can stay unset.
#
# Both are secrets. .env is gitignored and never reaches an image
# (.dockerignore excludes .env*), which is where the two blocks below differ
# from this template: a template carries names, never values.
#
# PAYMENT_PROVIDER=cutluy

# --- CutLuy -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Create a store and an API key under Dashboard -> API keys; the key starts
# with ck_live_ and identifies the store's payment link on its own.
# Leave CUTLUY_API_KEY unset to keep the payment routes answering 503.
# CUTLUY_BASE_URL=https://cutluy.com
# CUTLUY_API_KEY=
#
# The endpoint secret from Dashboard -> Webhooks, for the endpoint pointed at
# this API's /api/v1/payments/callback. Unset, deliveries are refused: nothing
# is ever fulfilled on a notification that is not signed. The counter can still
# close a sale by polling, so this is only required once CutLuy is told to post.
# CUTLUY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# How far a delivery's timestamp may stray from this clock before it is
# refused as a replay. CutLuy's own sample uses 300 seconds.
# CUTLUY_WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_SECONDS=300
#
# What the numbers on the shelf are. CutLuy charges USD only, so a KHR-priced
# shop has its lines converted at CUTLUY_KHR_USD_RATE — riel per dollar, e.g.
# 4100 — and the receipt still shows the riel prices the products carry. Set it
# to the rate the shop actually banks at and keep it current: it is a number
# the shop owns, not one this service can look up.
# CUTLUY_SHOP_CURRENCY=KHR
# CUTLUY_KHR_USD_RATE=
#
# How long to keep waiting for a customer before giving up, in seconds, if a
# payment somehow comes back without the gateway's own expiry. CutLuy's codes
# expire about five minutes after creation.
# CUTLUY_FALLBACK_EXPIRY_SECONDS=300

# --- ABA PayWay -------------------------------------------------------------
#   Register at https://sandbox.payway.com.kh/register-sandbox/ ; PayWay
#   emails a merchant id and an API key. The API key is the short string
#   PayWay's own samples call the "public key"; the RSA pair in the same
#   email belongs to the token-based "credentials on file" product and is not
#   used by the QR API.
#
#   PayWay also refuses any caller it has not whitelisted (gateway code 6),
#   so before the first successful request ask the PayWay integration team
#   to whitelist the public address the api container calls from.
#
# PAYWAY_BASE_URL defaults to the sandbox host. Production is
# https://checkout.payway.com.kh and needs real credentials.
# PAYWAY_BASE_URL=https://checkout-sandbox.payway.com.kh
# PAYWAY_MERCHANT_ID=
# PAYWAY_API_KEY=

# The currency charged to the customer: KHR or USD. KHR amounts must be whole
# riel and at least 100; USD amounts must be at least 0.01.
# PAYWAY_CURRENCY=KHR

# How long a displayed QR stays payable, in minutes. PayWay allows 3 minutes
# to 120 days; a counter wants a few minutes so a mistaken sale cannot be
# paid an hour later.
# PAYWAY_QR_LIFETIME_MINUTES=10

# Public HTTPS address of this API's payment callback, e.g.
# https://pos.example.kh/api/v1/payments/callback. Unset, the api polls
# PayWay's Check-transaction API when a cashier or client asks about a
# payment, which works on a laptop with no public address. Note that PayWay's
# notification carries no signature, so a callback is only ever a hint to go
# and check — it can never mark money received by itself.
# PAYWAY_CALLBACK_URL=

# PayWay's branded QR image variant (template1_color … template6). Unset asks
# for the plain code, which is what a terminal screen wants.
# PAYWAY_QR_IMAGE_TEMPLATE=
